Pre-Work Placement Requirements
1. Academic Requirements
Before starting a work placement, students must fulfil several academic requirements demonstrating the fundamental knowledge and skills required to make a significant contribution in a professional computing environment.
•	Completing foundational courses, including databases, web development, object-oriented programming, and software engineering concepts.
•	keeping up a satisfactory academic standing in accordance with the placement eligibility requirements set forth by the institution.
•	Completion of any pre-placement seminars or workshops on professional conduct, interview technique, and CV writing that the college or placement office hosts.
•	Submission of a study plan or placement learning agreement that details the learning goals for the placement term and how they relate to program outcomes.
•	Knowledge of version control systems like Git and proficiency in at least one programming language that is relevant to the industry.

2. Professional Expectations
Companies that host placement students have specific professional standards. Students should act appropriately from the start and recognise that a work placement is a formal employment relationship.
•	Reliability and punctuality include being on time, fulfilling deadlines, and promptly expressing any problems.
•	Professional communication involves adjusting tone to suit various audiences, including technical and non-technical stakeholders, and using suitable language in emails, meetings, and written material.
•	Collaboration and teamwork include assisting coworkers, taking part in stand-ups or sprint reviews (particularly in Agile contexts), and making a good contribution to team initiatives.
•	Initiative and self-management: exhibiting the capacity to oversee your own workload, seek clarification when necessary, and assume responsibility for activities without continual supervision.
•	Respecting workplace clothing requirements, IT usage guidelines, expectations for remote work, and other employer-established norms of conduct are examples of adhering to business policies.
•	Continuous learning involves actively seeking out feedback, interacting with mentors, and putting new technology and abilities learned throughout the placement to use.

3. Legal and Ethical Considerations
Placement students need to understand the ethical obligations and legal frameworks that regulate professional behaviour in the computer sector. In a professional context, ignorance of these responsibilities is not a valid justification.
•	Data Protection and GDPR: Students working with personal or sensitive data must understand and comply with the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). This includes handling data securely, not sharing it without authorisation, and reporting any breaches promptly.
•	Confidentiality and NDAs: Many employers will require placement students to sign a Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA). Students must respect the confidentiality of proprietary code, business processes, client information, and internal systems.
•	Intellectual Property (IP): Any work produced during the placement is typically owned by the employer. Students should clarify IP ownership arrangements and must not use or reproduce company code, designs, or data outside of work.
•	Health and Safety: Students are entitled to a safe working environment and must comply with the employer's health and safety procedures, whether working on-site or remotely.
•	Equality and Inclusion: Students must treat all colleagues and clients with respect, regardless of background, and are expected to uphold equality and diversity policies in line with employment law in Ireland and the UK (e.g. the Employment Equality Acts).
•	Ethical use of technology: Placement students must not engage in any unethical use of company systems, including unauthorised access, misuse of data, or use of AI tools in a way that violates company policy or professional standards.

